#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "da62e401-d03d-54fd-90b5-410d36bdefad" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1226" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3$." :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}{:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/695bd620f29881bf3d1d12a15a27402a.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}\n$$\n\nand shift it $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-2)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/06e822096d671208bbf430caf55e8190.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "be6b6b07-c139-5ba2-b463-420a2ead54c8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1227" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/3a95ed2ef7081272671f5a41923a47a2.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/6817eccf2c7e1306200e1729650b093f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,3)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/9f34b42a63bcf684cb04714ab7ee5292.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "5ab90279-394b-5df0-8bf3-a1042196b40c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1228" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y = k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$. Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/d8f79039924340963dd585e4535b185a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve\n\n$$\ny=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c\n$$\n\nis $y=c$. In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5$.\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y$. The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1)$, so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + c &= y \\\\\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} - 5 &= y \\\\\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{0} - 5 &= -1 \\\\\nk - 5 &= -1 \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "20a59278-1d09-5519-a362-4634ac9d5144" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1587" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{x} + c$. Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/54cd2e1f9633f7e5d5c1536079217c29.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x\n$$\n\nis $y = 0$.\n\nThe vertical translation\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+c\n$$\n\nshifts the asymptote to $y=c$.\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1$. Therefore, $c=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8e94192-12b8-5fb3-9b2c-24ad6146199e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243359} {:answer/id #uuid "4950a90f-d990-5237-a730-339e2f778a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243360} {:answer/id #uuid "1da6a60b-7ed8-5f86-ac74-a08fceabbe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361} {:answer/id #uuid "1a121cb3-97ae-5f85-88a5-b2c1c582ccf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243362} {:answer/id #uuid "4700703f-8be8-59d3-ab77-8245ecd49b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da6a60b-7ed8-5f86-ac74-a08fceabbe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243361} :answer-field/id #uuid "d558351d-dd08-592d-a83d-147f0983afeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243358}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a40fc808-0e04-5dbc-af3a-696d4fcf0822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243366} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2af229-5140-5a43-b7fa-c4a5d33b65f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243367} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1d4225-515a-5653-87ff-1da50cde4cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243368} {:answer/id #uuid "624170b7-c364-5634-b8a5-b464680480ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243369} {:answer/id #uuid "26156bcb-4775-5f66-a4aa-b5fd9ad3c34a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40fc808-0e04-5dbc-af3a-696d4fcf0822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243366} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b43d465-a0b4-535f-8262-fab967b6ad8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243365}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8624b867-0c46-5fb9-bd50-4d5ac46cd2c3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-170350" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567c3334-9626-55f9-9ca3-695a8a8c1bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243373} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b2f0c4-5568-58ef-8699-3a99af8146a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243374} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e20aa-9343-56f2-8671-6c9362171179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243375} {:answer/id #uuid "e577ad46-6c70-52ea-9816-b7ebe3cc1a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243376} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0a8758-828c-5cc9-b81f-7199613c1ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e20aa-9343-56f2-8671-6c9362171179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243375} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9fc2285-8e22-5fd0-892b-5258a60a59f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243372}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7240e13f-7aa1-5190-bc2b-38e383505724" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-27447" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/458e804bf960b7f2d117722693a993aaf5b25741bf7cd872e77a5daef7cfd66a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ What is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590f8d7e-9f87-5eb8-8adf-687615d77843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/d6034af21a31fd