emy-id "q-27477" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e48bb5f-f8c0-51a2-9e42-f3a951bedc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066258} {:answer/id #uuid "16ce6f74-a971-5e51-80f9-906e367f7387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066259} {:answer/id #uuid "215af631-845c-59fa-bb65-f18cb5bf2c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066260} {:answer/id #uuid "32737268-dd09-57a4-8aeb-64621275384e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066261} {:answer/id #uuid "70266bde-ab79-5c42-abf0-c4d154e65ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ce6f74-a971-5e51-80f9-906e367f7387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066259} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b9ea84-df56-5bb2-b4ac-3db48d048c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dd2a7c3-0fdf-5e47-bc41-0d52827cdf03" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-27479" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-27479.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y = (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} + c$. What is the value of $c$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccfeff70-7635-545e-840d-3e6adab357a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-27480-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066264} {:answer/id #uuid "ea50e082-fb1b-5d5f-8cf8-978fc98b1986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-27480-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066265} {:answer/id #uuid "178ddf33-749a-56dc-a704-b245c370e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-27480-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066266} {:answer/id #uuid "42b9c368-68c2-59ba-ad09-8343b1ac9c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-27480-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066267} {:answer/id #uuid "5274f58d-9eed-5d3a-bc62-30620135397f" :answer/type {