{:answer/id #uuid "c338bc12-0ff7-5a76-a030-be58a3ab86eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066477} {:answer/id #uuid "d00aa293-bf91-5633-90dd-7eed6a5053fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066478} {:answer/id #uuid "0acd9f5c-22c4-5c1b-b1c0-484d8e71bf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066479} {:answer/id #uuid "298b6955-f792-591c-917e-af9ab187a7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acd9f5c-22c4-5c1b-b1c0-484d8e71bf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066479} :answer-field/id #uuid "72552714-b712-5e79-9e14-0dd03e6c2a7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0772e5dc-a19a-5346-a868-136cb8608ad3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-31364" :question/problem "For the curve $y = \\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + \\frac{4}{3}$, find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130b240e-e849-5264-9d37-f2db7059c1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243429} {:answer/id #uuid "192ea508-b7c4-5d93-a334-f5060ffdeafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/749d466c80d05d054b8d661b6c3405c92f904df65ca7c3442746d19f73394834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243430} {:answer/id #uuid "75db1385-dd3c-5856-b311-b70a2f0d9c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243431} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ea120e-8c3b-5af4-915b-321f84aec15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/4441bcb0d11698cff76c8e74c7c34a8109523a581eb5810c388e9f17c09e8176.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243432} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7502c9-b2f0-510b-a317-dc36f9057624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6212d04b1004e0110d7aff817f03bab0b98f74dd5e334fe6607475ed13fe3fb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130b240e-e849-5264-9d37-f2db7059c1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243429} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0b30b2-a33a-573f-ab4e-249638505b81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243428}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32765731-a8b7-598c-bfe7-1dd2c33c7b72" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-31416" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ebbe2d-74ec-5380-9a07-702d026d313d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066482} {:answer/id #uuid "00accd83-f7f9-57bb-aa6e-78f38f349bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066483} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7e45dc-8e5a-5e38-a11e-07a10f544e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066484} {:answer/id #uuid "319a2339-b438-5f58-b459-e90c8e2d9866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066485} {:answer/id #uuid "7941c9a0-4881-5921-a1a9-38e914122b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7e45dc-8e5a-5e38-a11e-07a10f544e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/q-31424-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066484} :answer-field/id #uuid "375a8c1c-ecfc-5e8e-99de-762874c0a19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066481}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af553bc-4342-536e-bf61-1a65d8389d56" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-31424" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y = 4(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} - 2$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02a727f-56bb-5ad0-beda-3be135f65bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243436} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe1ea3b-69b8-5ff2-99d7-e79f47f68810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243437} {:answer/id #uuid "c66fa661-8570-5bb7-a48e-db51a4ad5d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243438} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e684c9-b7e5-551d-bec9-fbe1571b47e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243439} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6465c2-f502-5ca1-a4bb-b74d3f8f658f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66fa661-8570-5bb7-a48e-db51a4ad5d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243438} :answer-field/id #uuid "476b40d2-fac8-57a3-a5b1-24c906856425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e11eb136-331c-59a1-bd47-5813f0e994df" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-31435" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cea4abd-4e1a-5956-bb45-ae956c7dd9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243443} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f0d09d-f72a-524d-9024-ec3605a9b2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243444} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba6da0c-ba6c-56e5-9f01-2110340f21d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243445} {:answer/id #uuid "8f291ce2-f97d-52e7-a1bf-afea2d925af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243446} {:answer/id #uuid "110c18c9-6b7c-568b-8bd1-a7b91291f6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba6da0c-ba6c-56e5-9f01-2110340f21d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243445} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d4cfb6-3e51-5560-b60c-60851c45a897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebf541e4-55e3-5021-83f0-ef5b1063441e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/8bde9a87a568654933ce9996f23605ea1219e2ff8416d9d5097971d9b6ef6087.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Hence, $c=-1.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-1 & =4 \\\\ k-1 & =4 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=5-(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=5{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44185d00-c94c-5eca-adad-259c2ad03020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243450} {:answer/id #uuid "9d553817-6216-507e-a0fc-6d4a4e94b1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243451} {:answer/id #uuid "fa410941-bb5a-5021-a33c-67f9cab616ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243452} {:answer/id #uuid "eb98bfbe-ba9c-52b2-b114-8fd6ed29caa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243453} {:answer/id #uuid "c13df81d-ac9c-5dd0-b3d8-6e4cc5a50012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d553817-6216-507e-a0fc-6d4a4e94b1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243451} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20ca045-3967-5890-a857-d12308329bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "689f0bb6-ca76-5ace-9159-9fe3c07b1013" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7252" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/f7cb93e2aa4cede2739d43fc33fc01755b5848465d46fed764eef87a1f9d3df9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee49e73-e9b5-582c-b471-6e1230a38b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243457} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ecb5b-3e25-5867-85ef-2e22d01760f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243458} {:answer/id #uuid "cc98a234-ef1f-5a63-8efb-acd9df233cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243459} {:answer/id #uuid "5051d516-49f5-5992-be3f-26b72a0cc679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243460} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0fb25f-3c67-513e-9eac-30c21188bbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5051d516-49f5-5992-be3f-26b72a0cc679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243460} :answer-field/id #uuid "798db4cd-8f43-5ed3-bcf8-05917ec137aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243456}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80543b3-4997-5175-82f2-660b5af23e52" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-7253" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/50be1f4a894e86b5f06fba18655caca163c3d4c7a3423d2ecf159aa49975eb8c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}]}
